A||N||17||DreXteR||Porting Heads||||1||
Z||000000||DreXteR||05-31-2001||01:28 AM||||I saw that the fiero heads have a lump in the middle. I have been told when porting the heads DONT TOUCH THE LUMP or be ending in a performance DECREACE intead of adding power.  But I saw the SBC/LT1 performance heads dont have that lump!!  Why??<P>What that lump do?<P>Thanx<P>DreX||||reg||1
Z||000001||Indiana_resto_guy||05-31-2001||01:58 AM||||The 'lump' takes up space leaving less room for air, ie: higher compression.<BR>SBC/LT1's are set up differently and the piston makes up the difference with similar results.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000002||pontiacs4me||05-31-2001||03:05 AM||||Where is the lump, in the combustion chamber, or in one of the ports?  I can tell you that if it is in (wow thats alot of 2 letter words) the intake port, it is a restriction, and will cause the incoming air to make a detour, which slows down traffic, I mean airflow, and you make less power.  If it is in the exhaust port same thing, it just causes backpressure and restricts the incoming air essentially because the pressure within the exhaust is holding intake air back.  <BR>Now if it is in the combustion chamber there are a few scenarios which can occur.  #1 It will reduce your overall compression ratio, depending on the size of the bump, if it is large, leave it alone, if it is small you may want to remove it, especially if you are using other methods to increase the compression ratio.  (decking the heads, decking the block, shim head gasket, overbored block, etc). Because the bump may cause a hot spot in the combustion chamber, and will cause your engine to ping.  Depending on what your final combustion ratio is of course.  Does anybody have any pics of this bump that I can see.  I am pretty experienced in porting heads, but I am considered a rookie still.  But my friends 5.0 did pick up .42 seconds in the 1/4 after I ported his heads.||||reg||1
Z||000003||cadaver||05-31-2001||03:53 AM||||Shim the head gasket? Wont that lower the ratio not raise it?||||reg||1
Z||000004||vEnOm||05-31-2001||09:07 AM||||Sup DreX <P>pontiacs4me:<BR>Where is the lump, in the combustion chamber, or in one of the ports? <P>The lump is at the ports. Each port has a lump (very BIG lump). As far as I know the lump makes a swirl motion of the air fuel mixture. My heads are ported & polished and the guy who did it shaped the lump like a shark fin. I don't know if removing that lump will afect the swirl motion. <P>Hey DreX why don't you take a pic of your heads and I will take a pic of mine so people can compare and see the diferences.<P>vEnOm<BR>87 GT Nitrous Project||||reg||6
Z||000005||87GTZ34||05-31-2001||10:43 AM||||Drex,  Air is a funny thing to control and predict how it will act.  The engineers found that for the port size & shape, the floor bump helps to 'bend' the air into the 90 degree turn without creating turbulence.  Those of you that have removed it or modified it, have probably hurt the intake airflow at mid & high rpms.  The sister head with smaller valves produced 230 horses on a modified engine and the only rework on the heads was to smooth out casting surfaces.  Everything else they did (on the ports) resulted in lower engine output.  The conclusion was that head designers did a very good job with intake airflow on them.<p>[This message has been edited by 87GTZ34 (edited 05-31-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||Steven||05-31-2001||11:18 AM||||Here is a link to what my heads looked like after porting them. Sorry, was not able to post pictures from work, so the next best thing was a link to a previous post. <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum1/HTML/007407.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum1/HTML/007407.html</A> <P>------------------<BR> 85 Fiero GT<BR> 3.2 Liter Under Construction||||reg||1
Z||000007||theogre||05-31-2001||02:58 PM||||In short.... If something is pretty oviously cast into a GM head on purpose, You usually want to leave it there unless you know exactly what it does and how. GM wouldn't have put that there without reason. (It's a really big pain in the ____ to make that lump come out right in casting after casting...) The most you's want to do is smooth up any rough spots on it.<P>Generally it's a bad idea to make major changes to any head unless you have specific goals and experience and testing tools to reach them. For most street engines you'll get the best results simply smoothing things and basic port matching the intake.<P>Port matching the exhuast isn't always a good thing... you want to watch that one. It's been found in allot of setting that having the head opening smaller than the header/manifold opening creates an anti reversion effect and makes for better scavenging of the cylinder.||||reg||1
Z||000008||terryk||05-31-2001||03:04 PM||||The "vane" is what is responsible for the 17% improvement in flow of the heads. Leave it alone. I had the pleasure to talk with one of the engineers that worked on the HO engine and he stress "LEAVE THE VANE ALONE"||||reg||1
Z||000009||DreXteR||05-31-2001||06:29 PM||||Thanx for da info. Well I will smooth it intead of rip it out.<P><BR>Thanx all||||reg||1
Z||000010||terryk||05-31-2001||06:58 PM||||Don't touch the vane.||||reg||1
Z||000011||DreXteR||05-31-2001||07:37 PM||||Terryk - Eaven the vane cant be polished?<P><BR>Thanx<P>DreX ||||reg||1
Z||000012||Brucepts||05-31-2001||08:45 PM||||Polishing to a smooth finish will slow the velocity of the air down and in turn decrease the CFM's of that given port (can be seen on flowbench with a velocity probe). The finish you want is a slightly rough finish. A smooth finish effects boundry layer air, you want to keep the air along the walls of the port turbulent this keeps it moving instead of becoming stagnant. Also keeps fuel from puddling. Without a flowbench to tell you if you are making improvements porting is a stab in the dark to say the least. Yes you might stumble onto something that works in the high range but that affects low rpm torque. You want to find a happy medium for a street motor. Swirl and tumble are very important in these motors. <P>------------------<BR>Advanced Dealer in the Great Material Continuum . . .<BR>Non Fiero Website: <A HREF="http://tractorsport.com" TARGET=_blank>http://tractorsport.com</A> <BR>You can't race a dyno . . .||||reg||1
Z||000013||88formula||05-31-2001||08:57 PM||||You don't want to touch the vane in the intake opening. Don't even sharpen it or you will hurt your performance. All I did was remove some of the casting flash from the opening to help improve air velocity. I left the vanes shape alone keeping it round and high. I concentrated most of my efforts in the bowl area, which is where most of the gains can be found anyway.<P>That vane is responsible for a 17% airflow increase compared to a head without one because it bends the air over the sharp short side radius without turbulence.  <BR> <P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88formula_logo_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000014||Vader||05-31-2001||10:16 PM||||DreX,<P>If you want all the low-end torque the little engien can make, keep the GM casting imperfections - that's exactly what they are intended to provide.  If you want more flow @ RPM (i.e., horsepower) open up the bowl areas, enlarge the valves, raise the port ceilings, and repair the short-side radius on intake and exhaust ports.  Get a cam that supports the head flows and start hunting for unsuspecting V-6s.  This worked pretty well for me:<P> <IMG SRC="http://homestead.juno.com/jlstrey/files/Porting.jpg"> <P>------------------<BR>"<I>Let the bodies hit the floor!</I>"||||reg||1
Z||000015||terryk||06-01-2001||12:45 AM||||I remember him saying something like "go around it" or "avoid it". Something like that. I took that to mean to polish the rest of the port but not the vane. That's what I've done.<P>Not having a flow bench and enough heads to verify what he said I .......||||reg||1
Z||000016||vEnOm||06-01-2001||09:47 AM||||Hey terryk, so that means that im doomed?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"> The thing is I already ported the heads and the "vane" is smooth and it looks like a shark fin. My heads look like the ones that Steven has. <P>Has anyone done this porting and put the heads on a flow bench? How much is the flow before and after? I need to know before I close the engine out, I don't want to take the heads off again because they suck. <P>Please HELP!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif"><P>Venom<BR>87 GT Nitrous Project||||reg||8
Z||000017||Toddster||06-01-2001||12:33 PM||||DON'T TOUCH THAT LUMP!!!!!!!!!<P>Maybe it's been said already but you will have two very expensive paper weights if you grind those "lumps" down.<P>They are called vanes and they deflect the incoming air into a spiral to aid in fuel/air mixing and help direct the exhaust out the correct port. <P>Additionally, DON'T PORT S%*T. Polish only!!! Removing material from your exhaust ports will reduce backpressure too much. Your performance will take a nose dive.<P>There are only a couple of small places where chamber material can be removed. Don't waste time trying to fill a coffee can full of shavings. Just polish and port match and improve performance other ways, ie. springs, pistons, balancing, turbo, etc.||||reg||1
